Your class must show Class 5 students that touch can mislead but a thermometer does not. The school has no ice. You have three bowls, warm water from a kettle, tap water, cooler water from a matka (a thermometer shows it is 8 °C below the tap water) and one laboratory thermometer. One Class 5 child is sure that 'my left hand is just colder than my right hand'. Write:
(i) which water goes into which bowl, and why;
(ii) what the students will do with their hands;
(iii) your prediction;
(iv) one extra step that rules out the 'left hand is colder' idea;
(v) how the thermometer is used and the conclusion the students should reach.

A family has one digital clinical thermometer. Decide, with a reason each time:
(i) how to measure a healthy adult's temperature at home;
(ii) what to do before passing it to the next family member;
(iii) what to check first if the display stays blank when it is switched on;
(iv) whether a reading of 37.3 °C under the tongue of a healthy-feeling teenager must mean a fever;
(v) whether it can be used to check that the water in the kettle has boiled.

A laboratory has three thermometers.
X: range -10 °C to 110 °C, smallest division 1 °C.
Y: range 0 °C to 50 °C, smallest division 0.5 °C.
Z: range -30 °C to 50 °C, smallest division 2 °C.
Choose, with a reason:
(i) a thermometer for water boiling at about 98 °C;
(ii) a thermometer for a salt and ice mixture at about -20 °C;
(iii) a thermometer for warm water at about 37.5 °C;
(iv) the thermometer with the widest range (show the spans);
(v) why no single thermometer here is best for all three tasks.

Work backwards. Describe the markings (the gap between two bigger marks and the number of small divisions between them) of:
(i) a thermometer whose smallest division reads 2 °C;
(ii) a thermometer whose smallest division reads 0.5 °C;
and (iii) show that your answer to (ii) works by dividing.
